# Solution — GIAM 1.4 Problem 2

## Problem

Dividing an integer by two has an interesting interpretation in binary: simply shift the digits to the right. Thus $ 22 = 10110_2 $ divided by two gives $ 1011_2 = 8 + 2 + 1 = 11 $. How can you recognize a doubly-even integer from its binary representation?

## The Answer

A doubly-even integer is recognized at a glance:

$$$
\boxed{\;n \text{ is doubly-even} \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\; \text{its binary representation ends in } 00 \text{ (the last two bits are both } 0).\;}
$$$

## Why — From Shifting to Trailing Zeros

Build it up from the problem's own "shift right $ = $ divide by two" idea.

**Even $ \Leftrightarrow $ ends in $ 0 $.** When you shift a binary number right, the bit that "falls off" the end is the remainder upon dividing by $ 2 $. So the division is *exact* — the number is even — exactly when that last bit is $ 0 $. (This is the textbook's Theorem 1.4.2: an integer is even iff its units binary digit is $ 0 $.)

**Doubly-even $ \Leftrightarrow $ ends in $ 00 $.** Recall (Problem 1) that $ n $ is doubly-even iff $ n $ is even *and* its half $ n/2 $ is again even. In binary:

- $ n $ even means the last bit is $ 0 $; shifting right by one gives $ n/2 $.
- $ n/2 $ even means *its* last bit is $ 0 $ too — i.e. the *second-to-last* bit of the original $ n $ is $ 0 $.

So both of the bottom two bits of $ n $ must be $ 0 $: the binary representation ends in $ 00 $. Equivalently, since doubly-even $ = $ divisible by $ 4 = 2^2 $, and $ 2^2 = 100_2 $, a multiple of $ 4 $ must end in two zeros.

## Checking the Example

The problem's own number settles things instantly. $ 22 = 10110_2 $ ends in "$ 10 $", *not* "$ 00 $". So $ 22 $ is **even** (it ends in a $ 0 $) but **not doubly-even**. Indeed, shifting right once gives $ 1011_2 = 11 $, which is *odd* — its half is odd, so $ 22 $ fails the second halving. (And $ 22 = 4 \cdot 5.5 $ is not a multiple of $ 4 $; $ 22 \equiv 2 \pmod 4 $.)

## Picture — Read the Last Two Bits

!(100%)[Binary representations with the last two bits boxed; doubly-even ⟺ ends in 00](problem-2-binary.svg "Top: 4 = 100, 8 = 1000, 12 = 1100 all end in 00 (green) — doubly-even. 2 = 10, 6 = 110, 22 = 10110 end in 10 (red) — even but not doubly-even. Bottom: shifting right twice, 12 = 1100 drops 0 then 0 (doubly-even), while 22 = 10110 drops 0 then 1 (not).")

## Remark — The General Rule: Divisible by $ 2^k \Leftrightarrow k $ Trailing Zeros

The pattern continues perfectly. Shifting right $ k $ times divides by $ 2^k $ and discards the bottom $ k $ bits; the division is exact iff all $ k $ discarded bits were $ 0 $. So:

$$$
2^k \mid n \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\; \text{the binary representation of } n \text{ ends in (at least) } k \text{ zeros.}
$$$

| property         | divisible by | binary ends in |
| :---             | :---:        | :---:          |
| even             | $ 2 $        | $ 0 $          |
| doubly-even      | $ 4 $        | $ 00 $         |
| triply-even      | $ 8 $        | $ 000 $        |
| $ k $-times even | $ 2^k $      | $ k $ zeros    |

This is the binary mirror of Problem 1's "$ k $-times even" hierarchy. The number of **trailing zeros** in the binary expansion of $ n $ is exactly the **2-adic valuation** $ v_2(n) $ from Problem 1 — binary makes that quantity *visible*, just by counting the zeros at the right-hand end.

## Remark — Why Binary Is the Natural Notation Here

Recognizing divisibility by powers of $ 2 $ is *trivial* in binary (count trailing zeros) but fiddly in decimal. The decimal tests are:

- divisible by $ 2 $: last decimal digit is $ 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 $;
- divisible by $ 4 $: the number formed by the **last two** decimal digits is a multiple of $ 4 $ (e.g. $ 1{,}312 $ — "$ 12 $" is a multiple of $ 4 $ — so $ 4 \mid 1312 $);
- divisible by $ 8 $: the last *three* decimal digits form a multiple of $ 8 $.

These work, but require arithmetic on a two- or three-digit tail. In binary the very same facts become "is the last bit $ 0 $," "are the last two bits $ 0 $," "are the last three bits $ 0 $" — a pure *look*, no computation. The reason is that $ 10 = 2 \cdot 5 $ carries an *extra* factor of $ 5 $, muddying the powers of $ 2 $, whereas base $ 2 $ is built from powers of $ 2 $ alone. (This is the same base-sensitivity that governed terminating decimals back in Section 1.1.)

## Remark — Zero Again

The integer $ 0 $ is doubly-even (Problem 1), and the binary rule agrees: $ 0 = 0_2 $ is *all* zeros, so it certainly "ends in $ 00 $" — in fact it ends in arbitrarily many zeros, the binary echo of "$ 0 $ is infinitely even" ($ v_2(0) = \infty $). The number of trailing zeros of $ 0 $ is unbounded, matching that $ 2^k \mid 0 $ for every $ k $.

## Remark — How Computers Actually Test This

This trailing-bit characterization is not just elegant — it is exactly how real software checks divisibility by powers of $ 2 $. Using a **bitwise AND** with a mask of low $ 1 $-bits:

$$$
n \text{ is even} \;\Leftrightarrow\; (n \mathbin{\&} 1) = 0, \qquad n \text{ is doubly-even} \;\Leftrightarrow\; (n \mathbin{\&} 3) = 0,
$$$

where $ 3 = 11_2 $ masks off the bottom two bits. A processor computes $ n \mathbin{\&} 3 $ in a single instruction — far faster than an actual division — which is why "is this a multiple of $ 4 $?" is one of the cheapest tests a computer can perform. The mathematics of the exercise ("look at the last two bits") and the engineering of fast code ("mask the last two bits") are literally the same idea.
